<div class="page">
<header class="masthead">
<p class="eyebrow">Fortress revival &middot; 2026-10-13</p>
<h1>Coordinator Rewrite Plan</h1>
<p class="standfirst">This plan follows the method on record: your decision on the rewrite's goals, and your decision on how the skills are written and checked. It adds no step, rule or tool. Where the record is silent, or two entries pull different ways, it lists a decision for you with the default the record suggests.</p>
<p class="standfirst">Times are UTC, dates 2026. A cost is tokens written. A cost marked "estimate" isn't measured. The files to open: <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/coordinator-rewrite-plan.md">this plan</a>, <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/coordinator-shape.md">the map</a>, <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/rule-sort.md">sort A</a>, <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/rule-sort-b.md">sort B</a>, <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/duplicated-rules-evidence.md">the rules' origins</a>, <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/HELD.md">the held list</a>.</p>
</header>

<nav class="parts" aria-label="Sections">
<h2>Six sections</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="#sec1">The steps, in order</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec2">Where the record's order and its entries differ</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec3">The writer's brief, a draft</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec4">Your decisions</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec5">Points the record leaves to the coordinator</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec6">What stays out of the rewrite</a></li>
</ol>
</nav>

<div class="intro">
<p>Words used here:</p>
<dl class="gloss">
<div><dt>coordinator</dt><dd>Session that talks to you, briefs the workers and keeps the record.</dd></div>
<div><dt>record</dt><dd>Files under <code>explorations/</code> that hold the project's memory: FACTS (what's established), POSITIONS (your decisions), INDEX (one line per note), PLAN (the working plan), the boot note (what's in flight), the held list (your points held while you read) and the history files.</dd></div>
<div><dt>conduct rule</dt><dd>Rule about how the coordinator works. The map counts 73 of them, written in 249 places.</dd></div>
<div><dt>map</dt><dd>Note that describes the coordinator's records before the rewrite: where each kind of content lives, its size, its repeats and the open questions.</dd></div>
<div><dt>baseline interview</dt><dd>Eighteen questions that fresh Fable and Opus sessions answered with no record and no skill, to show what a model does by default.</dd></div>
<div><dt>sort</dt><dd>Note that puts each conduct rule in a bucket against the interview: done by default, kept, or a rule for workers. Sort A is the coordinator's, and you commented on its page. Sort B is an Opus worker's, made without reading A.</dd></div>
<div><dt>consolidation</dt><dd>Rewrite of FACTS or POSITIONS to the present state. Every cut sentence goes word for word to the history file, and a script checks that nothing was lost.</dd></div>
<div><dt>writer</dt><dd>Opus session that edits the skills. It runs headless, outside this session, so its prompt cache lasts an hour. A script starts and resumes it.</dd></div>
<div><dt>cold read</dt><dd>Reading of a rewritten skill by a fresh Opus agent given only the skill. It flags what would send its reader wrong or make it search, and edits nothing.</dd></div>
<div><dt>mode</dt><dd>Kind of work the coordinator switches into, such as running a batch.</dd></div>
<div><dt>core</dt><dd>Part of the skill that's always loaded.</dd></div>
<div><dt>trial boot</dt><dd>Test the map proposes: an old boot and a new boot answer one quiz on the project's state, scored without knowing which boot answered.</dd></div>
</dl>
</div>

<section id="sec1" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">1</span>The steps, in order</h2>
<p>Step 1 is done and took about 3.5M, measured. The steps after it come to about 6.6M more. INDEX's form, if you take it after the trial boot, and the two optional scouts would add up to 2M more. Every figure is an estimate except where a step says measured.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>FACTS's first pass: done.</strong><ul><li>Produced: FACTS cut from 303K to 258K characters, its history moved to the history file, no title left that no longer holds, every line reference re-read. The median entry stayed at 1.15K: the brief cut history and kept detail, so entries of a few lines, the goal you set on 10-12, weren't reached.</li><li>Who: seven Opus slices, then two Fable checks.</li><li>Cost: about 3.5M, measured.</li><li>From you: nothing.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The record-keeping part, by the <code>fortress-repo</code> method.</strong><ul><li>Produces: the record-keeping part of the coordinator skill. It says what a FACTS entry and a POSITIONS entry are, with a measure. It gives the three tiers and when each is read: the entry at boot and in briefs, the history when a fact's provenance is in question, the report when the detail is needed. It says how a worker's report becomes an entry. And it says FACTS and POSITIONS hold the project only, while how we work goes in the skill.</li><li>How, in order: (a) a first draft, with a trial on the ten largest entries before and after, on the page "Record Rule Draft"; (b) you comment on the trial's entries, as you did on the <code>fortress-repo</code> review page; (c) your comments become the part's principles, which you read once; (d) a cold read: a fresh worker given only the part consolidates one slice, checked for the shrinkage the measure sets; (e) a quiz, the method of the trial boot: a fresh reader given the consolidated slice, with the history and the reports to look things up in, answers the questions a brief would need, scored against the answers the old text gives, so a fact lost shows as a wrong answer, and the questions seed the trial boot's quiz; (f) the part goes to the skill writer as a finished piece of the skill.</li><li>Who: Opus workers for (a), (c), (d) and (e). The coordinator gathers your comments.</li><li>Cost: about 1.6M, an estimate: 0.5M for the draft and trial, now running, 0.3M for the principles, 0.5M for the cold read, 0.3M for the quiz.</li><li>From you: your comments on the page, and one reading of the principles.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>FACTS's second pass and POSITIONS, by the part.</strong><ul><li>Produces: every FACTS entry in the part's form, then POSITIONS the same way. POSITIONS keeps project decisions only, and its two sections on how we work wait until the skill holds their rules (decision 17). Provenance goes to the history files, and detail stays in the reports.</li><li>Who: Opus workers by slices. Each brief quotes the part and never restates it. One check pushes for the cut against the measure, and one guards against loss.</li><li>Cost: about 2.5M, an estimate. INDEX's form waits for the trial boot (decision 3).</li><li>From you: nothing more, once you've read the part.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>Your held points and the decisions before the writer.</strong><ul><li>Produces: an answer to each of your points of 10-12 and 10-13, and your choices in section 4.</li><li>Who: the coordinator, one ask per message.</li><li>Cost: under 0.1M, an estimate. Two Sonnet scouts are optional: one for the failures of five rules (decision 7), one for the origins you asked about (decision 9). Each would add about 0.5M, an estimate.</li><li>From you: your answers.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The writer's brief.</strong><ul><li>Produces: the brief in section 3, finished with your answers.</li><li>Who: the coordinator.</li><li>Cost: under 0.1M, an estimate.</li><li>From you: you read it before the writer runs, as you set on 10-12.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The writer's draft.</strong><ul><li>Produces: the new skill, a core and one part per mode, with a new sources file. Committed, not pushed.</li><li>Who: a fresh writer.</li><li>Cost: about 0.3M to 0.4M, an estimate. The writer's pass over every part of <code>fortress-repo</code> was about 366K by the coordinator's count.</li><li>From you: nothing. Check-ins 45 minutes apart watch it.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The check against the kept list.</strong><ul><li>Produces: each kept rule found in the draft, as a rule or a fact, or listed as lost.</li><li>Who: not on record (section 5, point 1).</li><li>Cost: about 0.1M, an estimate.</li><li>From you: nothing.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The trial boot.</strong><ul><li>Produces: evidence whether the draft's boot keeps your awareness: what's decided, what's in flight, what's measured, what mustn't be asked or measured again.</li><li>Who: not on record. The map proposes it.</li><li>Cost: about 0.7M, an estimate. A boot today takes about 0.25M to 0.32M of context.</li><li>From you: decision 5. The default puts it here.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The cold read.</strong><ul><li>Produces: flags with their confidence, the undefined terms, the traps, and the lint's output.</li><li>Who: an Opus reader with the standing cold-read brief.</li><li>Cost: about 0.1M to 0.2M, an estimate. A batch's cold read of one part was about 0.08M.</li><li>From you: nothing.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The fixes.</strong><ul><li>Produces: the draft with the flags fixed.</li><li>Who: the coordinator reviews the flags, and the writer fixes them.</li><li>Cost: under 0.1M, an estimate. Resumed within the hour, the writer reads its context from the cache: once it read 45K and wrote 109.</li><li>From you: nothing.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>Your reading.</strong><ul><li>Produces: the skill as you approve it.</li><li>Who: the coordinator publishes it as a page. You read each part whole and comment. The coordinator briefs each comment to the writer, who fixes it at the level of its paragraph or section. The coordinator keeps the page in step.</li><li>Cost: about 0.1M to 0.3M a round, an estimate. A writer woken after more than an hour writes its context again. Measured: a writer run as a subagent, woken after a day, wrote 297K.</li><li>From you: your reading time.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>Retire the old homes.</strong><ul><li>Produces: the protocol retired. Its worker rules that <code>fortress-repo</code> lacks go there. The README's rule sections, the boot note's standing lines and PLAN's three old rule sections are retired or folded in. The boot order points to the new skill.</li><li>Who: the writer for <code>fortress-repo</code>, with a cold read of the changed part. The coordinator for the record files.</li><li>Cost: about 0.1M to 0.2M, an estimate.</li><li>From you: decision 22. The record gives this step no place in the order. The default puts it after your reading, since the protocol is read at boot until the new skill holds its rules.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong><code>cloud-container</code>, by the <code>fortress-repo</code> method.</strong><ul><li>Produces: an outline of what changes in each part, for you and then the writer. Then the skill rewritten whole by the seven writing principles, a cold read, the fixes and your reading.</li><li>Who: an Opus worker for the outline, then the writer and a cold reader.</li><li>Cost: about 0.3M to 0.5M, an estimate. The skill is 16.5K bytes with its parts. <code>fortress-repo</code>'s was 149K.</li><li>From you: the outline's choices, and decisions 23 and 24.</li></ul></li>
</ol>
<p>Then batch 14. PLAN holds it until both skills are done.</p>
