<h2><span class="num">3</span>The writer's brief, a draft</h2>
<p>This draft speaks to the writer as "you" and calls you "the curator". The coordinator finishes it with your answers to section 4. A line that waits on a decision names it. The paths are in the references, and the coordinator puts them inline in the brief it sends. It goes after <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/skill-writer-brief.md">the writer's standing brief</a>, and the draft is checked as <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/skill-cold-read-brief.md">the cold-read brief</a> says.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Your task.</strong> Write the <code>coordinator</code> skill from scratch. Its reader is the coordinating session, which talks to the curator, briefs the workers and keeps the record. The skill is that session's one home for how it works. It replaces the protocol and the README's rule sections. Write it in place, in the skill's folder. Git keeps the earlier text. Commit, and don't push. The skill holds what the model wouldn't do by itself, in this environment and this project. It keeps every role: executive assistant, batch coordinator, roadmap architect, briefer of workers and analyst. It says plainly what goes where in the record. It gives a lighter boot that keeps the curator's awareness: the coordinator knows the project without him, never asks a decision again, and never sends a worker to measure what's on record. FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX keep serving the workers' briefs.</li>
<li><strong>What you get.</strong><ul><li>Your standing brief, then this one, in a fresh session.</li><li>The method: the curator's decision on the rewrite's goals, and his decision on how skills are written and checked.</li><li>The rule texts: POSITIONS's sections on how we work and on how he wants to be spoken to, as consolidated, and the protocol. A rule with neither home has its line in the README, FACTS or the boot note, as the map lists it.</li><li>The kept list and the facts, in points 6 and 7.</li><li>The map, whole: the records, their repeats, the boot's size, the open questions, and where each home came from.</li><li>The origins of the most duplicated rules: the pattern and the record's words on duplication, and the section of any such rule you write.</li><li>The two sorts, for their facts and their proposed shapes, not for any rule's wording.</li><li>The seven writing principles.</li><li>The curator's points on the rules, from the held list, and the boot note's evidence paragraph.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>What you don't get.</strong><ul><li>The existing skill. Read no file in its folder, its sources file included.</li><li>FACTS, INDEX and the transcripts, unless a task names an entry.</li><li>The register of what you read. The record is written in agent shorthand.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>First, the modes.</strong> Identify the coordinator's modes before you write any rule. For each mode, describe the work, what starts it, and what it needs loaded. The record names these candidates:<ul><li>Running and reviewing a batch: the launch on his go, the watch, the landing, the review after it.</li><li>Briefing and running workers outside a batch: the tier, the brief, watching a long run.</li><li>Keeping the record: consolidation, the history files, INDEX.</li><li>Rewriting a skill: the writer, the cold read, his comments on a page.</li><li>Process engineering: measuring how the work runs and what it costs.</li><li>A post-mortem: finding what happened and why, from git and the transcripts.</li><li>Planning the road: PLAN's phases, and the forks probed before a batch is briefed.</li><li>Talking with him, asking and deciding aren't a mode. They're the core.</li><li>Put each of the five roles in the core or a mode (decision 6).</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The shape.</strong><ul><li>A core, always loaded, and one part per mode, loaded when the coordinator switches into it.</li><li>The core holds the facts of point 7 and a few general principles. The standing goal comes first: model what the curator knows and fill his gaps with the facts he needs to decide. Then his right to decide, how to talk to him in each situation, what goes where in the record, and the boot.</li><li>Each mode's part holds the kept rules of that work.</li><li>Concepts first, as a dictionary, then short procedures.</li><li>Keep the whole skill no longer than the 41K bytes of the skill it replaces, its sources file aside. Sort A puts the core at about 10K bytes.</li><li>Organize the record as the model organizes knowledge by itself. The curator calls this the rewrite's highest risk. The interview showed the model's own shape: a short current-state file, with decisions and history apart. That shape needs no instruction.</li><li>A rule that binds workers belongs in <code>fortress-repo</code>, not here.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The kept rules.</strong> A rule is kept when the failure it guards against is on record. The interview alone drops nothing. Write each rule from its text in POSITIONS or the protocol.<ul><li>Talking and asking:<ul><li>Ask one thing per message, in the context its text gives (decision 9).</li><li>An ask gives the question, the context, the options, what a yes commits him to and what a no costs. The recommendation comes last.</li><li>Don't use the dialog tool. Its prompt times out after about 60 seconds in the iOS client, so the rule lapses if the client changes.</li><li>Use lists, not tables. The iOS client shows a table as one scrolling line.</li><li>Write numbers as K or M: 5K, 1.2M.</li><li>Define a term that's new to him where you use it.</li><li>Read the clock before writing any time (decision 12).</li><li>Say nothing of a running batch until it lands or fails (decision 16).</li><li>Record edits: decision 11.</li><li>Answer the git-check hook's reminders silently (decision 24).</li><li>While he reads turn by turn, restate each of his points in a line and hold it. Reply when he says he's done. This guards against the wall-of-text spiral.</li><li>Send a document for his approval as a page, with a Select button per larger section and the references at the bottom (decision 21).</li></ul></li><li>Deciding:<ul><li>His role: decision 10. A bare go starts background work only. A batch run waits for his explicit go. For the phase-3 batches, his standing go is that go. A standing approval covers only what it names.</li><li>Search the record before you ask him anything or measure anything.</li><li>A decision inside a worker's report isn't made until he sees it. Write it from POSITIONS's text on which decisions reach him and how. <code>fortress-repo</code> tells workers which choices are theirs.</li><li>What makes a decision consequential, and the three ways it reaches him.</li><li>A reversible stop doesn't hold a batch (decision 7).</li><li>When something goes wrong, take a deeper pass, not a halt or a rollback.</li><li>Weigh a rule against its purpose and its cost.</li><li>A remark isn't a decision. A go is written nowhere.</li></ul></li><li>Delegating and briefing:<ul><li>Give reading to workers. Read their reports and the record.</li><li>Probe a fork before a batch is briefed (decision 7).</li><li>Give idle time to parked research. Discuss a new deliverable with him first.</li><li>A brief states the problem, not the expected answer.</li><li>A brief points at the research on file.</li><li>A brief hands over the record's measurements as findings.</li><li>A clean worker is clean of our options, not of our measurements.</li><li>Which tier runs what, the Fable rule, and a two-area decision in two steps: cheap evidence, then top-tier judgement.</li><li>Watch a long run against its estimate. The spend is the coordinator's to watch.</li><li>Give estimates in the project's units. A token count counts writes only.</li><li>The writer makes every skill edit, and a cold read follows. A comment marks a problem area.</li></ul></li><li>Boot and the record:<ul><li>The boot order. The trial boot settles what's read whole and what's looked up (decision 5).</li><li>FACTS read whole at boot (decision 7).</li><li>No directory listings at boot, and every output bounded (decision 7).</li><li>Whether a worker still runs: the harness's notice, and a check on its one output path.</li><li>What's in flight is read from the tree (decision 7).</li><li>The boot note is rewritten whole, never appended to.</li><li>One home per thing. His words are written once, in POSITIONS.</li><li>FACTS and POSITIONS state the present. History goes to the history files.</li><li>Consolidate before each batch. Run the check that nothing was lost before the commit.</li><li>Cite an entry by its bold title. A title that no longer holds is rewritten, its old text kept in the history and every citation updated.</li></ul></li><li>Dropped at his word: the line about the work-in-progress branches.</li><li>Dropped, no failure on record (decision 8):<ul><li>Label which part of a reply answers which.</li><li>Take a step a yes covers without asking again.</li><li>A fact enters in the commit that establishes it.</li><li>"Search the record for how it was done last time" folds into the search above.</li></ul></li><li>New, at his word only (decision 14):<ul><li>No do-nothing option among the choices.</li><li>A brief for an update caps its length against the version before.</li></ul></li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The facts stated instead of rules.</strong><ul><li>He reads on an iPhone, often with iOS Speak at a fast rate, and writes by dictation. A repository path doesn't open there. A page link or a GitHub link does. His client can show stale state, and the tree is the truth.</li><li>His time zone is Central European. The container's clock is UTC.</li><li>The context is compacted when it grows long. The container can be lost, and only what's pushed survives.</li><li>The record lives in the repository.</li><li>The session runs in auto mode. Its permission check can refuse a step after his go.</li><li>The main session's prompt cache lives an hour, a subagent's five minutes, a headless session's an hour. The Workflow harness runs two agents at once on this machine.</li><li>The platform stops the session's process about every 13 hours. Check-ins armed with <code>send_later</code> wake the session. They're spaced 45 minutes apart.</li><li>A message he sends during a turn is queued. A stop during a turn kills the running agents. A stopped Workflow run resumes with <code>resumeFromRunId</code>, keeping its finished agents. A subagent killed by an interrupt is relaunched only at his ask.</li><li>A Workflow <code>agent()</code> call can return null after its output. A Workflow script is checked as an async body, not with <code>node --check</code>.</li><li>The git-check hook asks for a commit after every turn. Its reminders are advisory.</li><li>Cached reads are almost free on his plan, so a cost counts tokens written only.</li><li>The tiers: the coordinating session and the workers run on Opus. Sonnet does archaeology, and nothing he reads. Fable judges a design question that spans two areas, and runs the reviews he pre-approved. Any other Fable work needs his yes.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The failures the kept rules guard against.</strong><ul><li>Time written from feel: "yesterday" for that day's recovery (09-18); "yesterday morning" (09-23); "an hour before" for three minutes (10-12); "01:0x" for a commit at 00:52, and two more times inside the very command that read the clock (10-13). On 10-12 the rule stood in four files: the protocol, POSITIONS, the skill and the boot note. Tonight's slips came under Opus.</li><li>A decision asked again: twice on 08-23, and the library question on 09-21. On 09-28 a clean worker and a Fable judgement, about 0.7M, were briefed on a question he'd settled at 19:06.</li><li>The search didn't fire before measuring: on 10-12 at 16:43 the coordinator measured entry lengths that the map already held, three minutes after reading INDEX's line on the map.</li><li>Work not delegated: the repository traced by hand on 09-19, after earlier cases.</li><li>The boot note appended to 79 times in a day.</li><li>Progress reports during a batch: two check-in reports in a row, and overnight runs that left him a session of progress notes.</li><li>Spend not watched: a probe wrote 7.0M against a stated 0.5M. A brief once asked a worker to cap its own spend.</li><li>The wrong tier: two workers cost 1.7M.</li><li>Choices that offered a do-nothing option, on 10-12 at 16:45.</li><li>The wall-of-text spiral. Both models' default is to act on each of his answers as it comes.</li><li>Tables, which both models gave him.</li><li>A document update that grew beyond what he asked, and a brief that reframed a document by naming a second reader (10-13).</li><li>A compaction that left him "out of control, unaware of crucial details".</li><li>Of the nine failures that led to a new copy of a rule, four broke a rule that already stood in a file read at boot.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>The writing standard.</strong><ul><li>Your standing brief: ASD-STE100 at least halfway, terms defined first, the glossary form, positive phrasing, and no rule that changes nothing. Don't take the register of what you read.</li><li>The seven writing principles.</li><li>The register the skill teaches for talking to him: plain short sentences with contractions, no flourish, no coda after a semicolon, a choice numbered so he can answer "3b", and no internal shorthand.</li><li>Provenance goes in the sources file, as your standing brief says.</li><li>No model identifier anywhere. Name models by tier.</li><li>Run the skills lint before each commit.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>How your draft is checked.</strong><ul><li>A check against the kept list: each kept rule found in the draft, or listed as lost.</li><li>The trial boot, if decision 5 puts it here.</li><li>The cold read, by a reader given only the skill, reading it as the coordinating session would.</li><li>The coordinator reviews the flags, and you fix them.</li><li>The curator reads each part whole, on a page. A comment marks a problem area. The coordinator briefs each comment to you, and you fix it at the level of its paragraph or section. The coordinator settles your minor decisions not taken from the record. He's asked only where a rule would change its meaning or a new rule would enter.</li></ul></li>
<li><strong>Your report.</strong> As your standing brief says: the commits, each file's size before and after with what changed, the decisions not taken with their alternatives, and anything you couldn't follow.</li>
</ol>
